<p>Our office is in Belton, so Bell County is where we live and work. For property in Belton, Killeen, Temple, Harker Heights, Nolanville, Salado or Copperas Cove, we can usually inspect quickly after a storm, attend the carrier's inspection in person, and come back for re-inspections without scheduling around a long drive. Joseph Dittman is our licensed public adjuster (Texas license #3356839).</p>

<h2>Hail and wind are the main claims here</h2>
<p>Central Texas gets severe spring storms with large hail and strong straight-line winds. Most Bell County claims we handle are roof claims on composition shingle homes, along with damaged siding, gutters, window screens, AC units, fences and the occasional tree on a roof. The carrier's usual arguments are familiar: the damage is wear and tear, the hail was too small, the dents are cosmetic, or you reported too late.</p>
<p>Being nearby helps in a specific way. We can get on your roof soon after the storm, before more weather muddies the evidence, and mark and photograph test squares on every slope. We write the estimate in Xactimate, so it can be compared with the carrier's estimate line by line. For more on how hail claims are judged, see <a href="/post/hail-damage-insurance-claim/">our hail damage insurance claim guide</a> and <a href="/services/roof-hail-damage-claims/">roof and hail damage claims</a>.</p>

<h3>Fences, trees and outbuildings</h3>
<p>Straight-line wind in Bell County often takes down wood fences, drops limbs on roofs and damages sheds and carports. Fences and detached buildings usually fall under "other structures" coverage, which has its own limit. Many policies also limit what they pay to remove a fallen tree, and some pay only when the tree hit a covered structure or blocks a driveway. Photograph the tree where it landed before it's cut up, and keep the tree service invoice.</p>

<h2>For Fort Cavazos families: handling a claim from somewhere else</h2>
<p>Military households near Fort Cavazos often PCS to a new duty station or deploy while still owning a home in Killeen, Harker Heights or Copperas Cove. If that home is damaged while you're away, you still have to report the claim, give the carrier access, document the damage and review the estimate. We can do most of the on-site work for you.</p>
<h3>If you've moved on PCS orders</h3>
<p>We inspect the property, photograph and measure the damage, and send you the documentation and our estimate. We meet the carrier's adjuster at the house, so you don't need to fly back for the inspection. You stay in the loop by phone, text or email, and you approve decisions on the claim.</p>
<h3>If you're deployed</h3>
<p>Communication may be limited. Tell us how and when you can be reached, and whether a spouse or family member will be your point of contact. If someone else will act on your behalf, check with the insurer about what authorization it requires from them.</p>
<h3>If the house is now a rental</h3>
<p>Your tenants' belongings are on their own renters policy, not yours. Your landlord policy covers the building and, if you bought it, lost rent. Ask your tenants to send photos of damage the day it happens, and read your policy's vacancy terms if the house sits empty between tenants.</p>

<h2>What we handle remotely, and what stays with you</h2>
