<h2>After a storm in Bell County</h2>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Check from the ground first.</strong> Look for dented gutters and downspouts, torn screens, damaged AC fins, and granules collected at downspout outlets.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph hail and damage the same day.</strong> If you're away, ask a neighbor or tenant to send photos with dates.</li>
<li><strong>Stop leaks.</strong> Tarp the roof and dry wet areas. Keep every invoice.</li>
<li><strong>Read your deductible.</strong> Many policies here use a percentage wind and hail deductible; check the declarations page.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim.</strong> Note the claim number, the adjuster's name and the date you reported. Insurers must acknowledge the claim and begin investigating within 15 days.</li>
<li><strong>Call before you sign anything with a roofer.</strong> Texas law bars a contractor from acting as your public adjuster on property they're repairing.</li>
</ol>
<p>The documentation checklist lists what to collect for hail, wind, water and fire losses, and it's printable for a tenant or family member to use.</p>
